;;; fold-family.slatl — Motoi Scheme Reference · broken-examples · the fold family
;;;
;;; A broken-example is kept ON PURPOSE. It is teaching signal: the code that
;;; does NOT run, why it does not run, the fix, the lesson, and the same idea
;;; expressed in three other libraries. This one teaches the single most common
;;; cross-standard trap in Scheme — the fold family — and through it the
;;; difference between R5RS, R6RS, Racket, and the SRFIs.
;;;
;;; Provenance for the DECISION: Motoi's standard is R7RS-small + SRFI parts,
;;; no R6RS, no Racket (see CLAUDE.md "The standard library"). fold-left / foldl
;;; / foldr are therefore NOT wired. Every reference program that reached for
;;; them is a broken-example, rewritten to SRFI-1 fold / fold-right.

(dialect :applies ("motoi") :note "broken-example — the fold family across standards")

(broken-example :name "fold-left"
  :library "broken-examples"
  :teaches "the-fold-family"
  :std-target "SRFI-1"
  :std-rejected ("R6RS" "Racket")
  :training-eligible #t
  :provenance "scheme/reference/broken-examples/fold-family.slatl"

  ;; ─── THE BROKEN CODE ───
  :broken-code "(display (fold-left + 0 (list 1 2 3)))"
  :broken-output "error: unbound symbol: fold-left"
  :runs "no — unbound symbol"

  ;; ─── WHY IT IS BROKEN ───
  :why
"`fold-left` is an R6RS name, not part of Motoi's standard. Motoi is
R7RS-small plus a set of named SRFI parts — no R6RS, no Racket. So `fold-left`
was never bound, and the program stops at the reader's first attempt to look up
the symbol. The mistake is not a typo; it is a *standard mismatch*. A programmer
who learned Scheme from an R6RS book will type `fold-left` by muscle memory and
be surprised it is missing. That surprise is exactly what this example exists to
convert into understanding."

  ;; ─── THE FIX ───
  :fix-code "(display (fold + 0 (list 1 2 3)))"
  :fix-output "6"
  :fix-runs "yes"
  :fix-note
"Use SRFI-1 `fold`. It is Motoi's standard fold and it lives in the default
bundle (R7RS-small + SRFI-1). No import needed."

  ;; ─── THE LESSON: same name, DIFFERENT argument order ───
  :lesson
"The trap is deeper than a missing name: even where a fold exists, the two
traditions pass the arguments to your function in OPPOSITE order.

  SRFI-1 `fold`   calls (kons ELEMENT accumulator)   — element first
  R6RS  `fold-left` calls (proc accumulator ELEMENT)  — accumulator first

For a commutative operation like + the result is the same (6 either way). For a
NON-commutative operation the results diverge, and silently:

  SRFI-1:  (fold - 0 (list 1 2 3))       => 2     ; (- 3 (- 2 (- 1 0)))
  R6RS:    (fold-left - 0 (list 1 2 3))  => -6    ; (- (- (- 0 1) 2) 3)

So porting R6RS code by blind find-replace of `fold-left`->`fold` is a BUG for
any non-commutative kons. You must also flip the lambda's parameter order. This
is why Motoi picks one fold and teaches its shape, rather than shipping both and
letting them collide."

  ;; ─── THE SAME IDEA IN THREE OTHER LIBRARIES ───
  ;; Each row: how this exact reduction is written elsewhere, so the reader can
  ;; map their prior knowledge onto Motoi's standard.
  :three-other-libs (
    (:lib "R5RS (1998)"
     :status "no fold at all"
     :code "(define (fold-left f acc xs)\n  (if (null? xs) acc\n      (fold-left f (f acc (car xs)) (cdr xs))))\n(display (fold-left + 0 (list 1 2 3)))"
     :note "R5RS shipped NO fold. Everyone hand-rolled one, and each codebase
picked its own argument order — which is the historical root of the whole mess.
The SRFIs and R6RS were competing attempts to standardize what R5RS left out.")
    (:lib "R6RS (2007)"
     :status "fold-left / fold-right, accumulator-first"
     :code "(display (fold-left + 0 (list 1 2 3)))   ; => 6\n(display (fold-left cons '() (list 1 2 3))) ; => (((()  . 1) . 2) . 3)"
     :note "R6RS blessed `fold-left` with (proc acc elem). Motoi rejects R6RS,
so this is exactly the code that does not run here. Kept as the counter-example.")
    (:lib "Racket"
     :status "foldl / foldr, element-first BUT list-last"
     :code "(display (foldl + 0 (list 1 2 3)))   ; => 6\n(display (foldl cons '() (list 1 2 3))) ; => (3 2 1)"
     :note "Racket's `foldl` is (proc elem acc) — element-first like SRFI-1 —
but Racket also swaps the argument POSITIONS of the seed and list versus some
libraries, and supports multi-list folds with a different arity rule. Close to
SRFI-1 but not identical; Motoi does not ship it, to avoid a near-miss lookalike.")
  )

  ;; ─── THE MOTOI ANSWER, RESTATED ───
  :motoi-standard
"Motoi ships exactly ONE fold family: SRFI-1 `fold` and `fold-right`, both
element-first `(kons elem acc)`. They are in the default bundle. `reduce` is
also SRFI-1 but note Motoi's current `reduce` is accumulator-first and is
flagged for conformance review (see std-buckets). If you want the R6RS shape,
you write it yourself with an explicit lambda — the language will not hand you a
second, subtly-different fold under a familiar name.")
